            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"MATCH\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical exegesis, fish primarily symbolize people gathered into God's kingdom through evangelism ('fishers of men') or for judgment (the dragnet). In extra-biblical historical tradition, the fish is also a dominant symbol for Jesus Christ via the ICHTHYS acrostic.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Identifies fish as men in the sea of nations, specifically the redeemed drawn out of the world as the catch of the fishers of men.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"People/souls caught for the kingdom (exegetical); Jesus Christ (traditional/iconographic).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Men in the sea of nations and the redeemed drawn out of it.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"No material boundary disagreement regarding the biblical usage. The book omits the extra-biblical ICHTHYS (Christ) acrostic, but the consensus already distinguishes between the symbol's exegetical meaning (people) and its historical/iconographic meaning (Christ).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's identification of fish as men/the redeemed caught in a net directly matches the consensus view of the biblical meaning of fish, centering on Jesus' call to be 'fishers of men' and the parable of the dragnet.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"It could be argued that the book completely ignores the widely recognized Christological meaning (ICHTHYS), which some consensus responses list as the absolute primary symbol, though they concede it is not derived from direct biblical exegesis.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
